    36	
    37	Primary source: Guccione–Guccione–Valqui, *On the shape of possible
    38	counterexamples to the Jacobian Conjecture*, arXiv:1401.1784v3, Theorem
    39	2.6, with Remark 2.5 and endpoint conventions 1.5–1.8. The local primary
    40	text `box/census-coverage-20260905/core-ggv-layout.txt` has SHA-256
    41	`e3694dde3f83c2ab6ed8d957fc6b53472e6a6dd55486af1dff345eade39e37b1`.
    42	Read the entire theorem statement/proof, its supporting §§1–2 through
    43	that proof, and notes [4]–[5]; also read Proposition 4.7 and note [14]
    44	for historical comparison. Theorem 2.6's existence result, including
    45	its cited upstream lemma, is retained as EXTERNAL-THEOREM, not reproved.
    46	
    47	The precise part used: for a polynomial Keller pair P,Q, a primitive
    48	integer direction `(rho,sigma)` with `rho+sigma>0` and `v(P)>0` admits
    49	a polynomial homogeneous Euler element E of weight `rho+sigma`, with
    50	
    51	```text
    52	[E,ell P]=ell P,
    53	st(P) parallel st(E), or st(E)=(1,1).
    54	```
    55	
    56	The polynomial clause is Theorem 2.6(1), not its Laurent-only conclusion.
    57	The direction need not have rho>0: `(0,1)` lies in V_{>0} and is allowed.
    58	For these directions st is the support point maximizing `i-j` on the
    59	face. Remark 2.5 says that monomial E forces `E=cAB` and, since the
    60	weight sum is positive, ell P monomial. None of these statements has a
    61	minimal-counterexample hypothesis. Note [14] already uses an analogous
    62	linear Euler element for a translation; its minimal-pair setting is not
    63	silently imported into the argument below.
    64	
    65	## 2. Upper-envelope lemma from the total leading monomial
    66	
    67	**Lemma.** Let P belong to a polynomial Keller pair over a characteristic-
    68	zero field. Suppose its total leading form is the single monomial
    69	`c A^a B^b`, with `c!=0`, `a,b>0`. Then `deg_B P=b` (and, by swapping
    70	the variables, `deg_A P=a`).
    71	
    72	Suppose a support point `(i,j)` has j>b. Unique total leading form gives
    73	`i+j<a+b`, hence i<a and
    74	`0<(j-b)/(a-i)<1`. Choose the maximum of these finitely many rational
    75	ratios, and write it in lowest terms as `rho/sigma`. Thus
    76	`0<rho<sigma`. For the weight `rho i+sigma j`, `(a,b)` ties with at least
    77	one of the selected points and dominates all support points:
    78	points with i<=a,j<=b are immediate; when i>a,j<b the strict total-
    79	degree inequality remains strict after replacing the A-weight 1 by
    80	`rho/sigma<1`; the remaining points are covered by maximality.
    81	
    82	Consequently this is an exposed nonmonomial face with start `(a,b)`;
    83	its other points have smaller A and larger B coordinates. Explicitly
    84	the direction along that face is a positive multiple of
    85	`(-sigma,rho)`, which has positive cross product with `(rho,sigma)`
    86	and agrees with the paper's counterclockwise endpoint convention.
    87	The weight of P is `rho*a+sigma*b>0`, so Theorem 2.6 applies.
    88	
    89	Any polynomial monomial `A^u B^v` of weight `rho+sigma` has v<=1.
    90	If v=1, then u=1; if v=0, then u=1+sigma/rho must be an integer.
    91	Thus E has support contained in `{(1,1),(1+sigma/rho,0)}`. If only one
    92	term occurs, Remark 2.5 contradicts the nonmonomial P face. Therefore
    93	both occur (in particular rho=1 by primitivity), and
    94	
    95	```text
    96	st(E)=(1+sigma/rho,0) != (1,1).
    97	```
    98	
    99	Theorem 2.6(2) now forces this point to be parallel to `(a,b)`, impossible
   100	because b>0. This contradiction proves `deg_B P<=b`; the leading
   101	monomial gives equality. The swap argument has Keller bracket changed
   102	only by a nonzero sign. No simple-root assumption occurs anywhere.
   103	
   104	## 3. The top B-face is a single shifted power when b>a
   105	
   106	Write `ell_(0,1) P=p(A)B^b`. By the lemma, `deg p=a`, with leading
   107	coefficient c. Apply Theorem 2.6 at `(0,1)`: its polynomial Euler
   108	element necessarily is `E=e(A)B`, where e is a nonzero polynomial.
   109	With the convention `[R,S]=R_A S_B-R_B S_A`, the identity is exactly
   110	
   111	```text
   112	b e' p-e p'=p.                                         (ODE)
   113	```
   114	
   115	Assume b>a>0. If e has degree zero, the left side has degree a-1,
   116	contradiction. If `r=deg e>=2`, its highest coefficient is
   117	`lc(e)*c*(br-a)!=0`, and degree `a+r-1>a`, again a contradiction.
   118	Hence `e=alpha A+beta`, and comparison of degree a gives
   119	`alpha=1/(b-a)`. Setting `s=beta/alpha`, equation (ODE) becomes
   120	
   121	```text
   122	(A+s)p'=a p,    hence p=c(A+s)^a.                       (TOP)
   123	```
   124	
   125	The last implication follows by translating A+s to a new variable
   126	and comparing all polynomial coefficients; it includes all root
   127	multiplicities and works over K itself. In particular no extraction
   128	of roots or field extension is needed. The translation parameter is
   129	also `s=[A^(a-1)]p/(a*c)`.
   130	
   131	## 4. Simultaneous F,G normalization and fixed-face preservation
   132	
   133	Apply §§2–3 to F with `(a,b)=(24,84)`. It follows that
   134	
   135	```text
   136	deg_B F=84,   ell_(0,1) F=(A+s)^24 B^84.
   137	```
   138	
   139	Apply §2 to G: `deg_B G=56` and
   140	`ell_(0,1) G=q(A)B^56`, where q has degree 16 and leader 1.
   141	The maximal possible B-weight of their bracket is `84+56-1=139>0`.
   142	Since the actual bracket is a nonzero constant, its weight-139 part
   143	must vanish, equivalently (also Proposition 1.13)
   144	
   145	```text
   146	56 p' q-84 p q'=0,     p=(A+s)^24.
   147	```
   148	
   149	Cancel the nonzero polynomial `(A+s)^23` in this polynomial identity.
   150	It gives `(A+s)q'=16q`, whence `q=(A+s)^16`. Thus both components
   151	have the SAME translation parameter, not independently chosen roots.
   152	
   153	Let `tau(A)=A-s`, `tau(B)=B`. This polynomial automorphism has Jacobian
   154	one, and
   155	
   156	```text
   157	ell_(0,1) tau(F)=A^24 B^84,
   158	ell_(0,1) tau(G)=A^16 B^56.
   159	```
   160	
   161	Translation creates no higher B powers. Each replacement term in
   162	`(A-s)^i B^j` has A-exponent <=i, with equality only for the original
   163	monomial. Therefore it preserves both unique total leaders. It also
   164	strictly lowers `(4,-1)` weight by `4k` for each k>=1 decrease in the
   165	A-exponent; hence both complete fixed `(4,-1)` faces are unchanged.
   166	This includes all numeric endpoint and edge coefficients, not just
   167	the direction. The original highest-A faces remain unchanged as well.
   168	In source coordinates `X=A-B, W=B`, tau is simply `X -> X-s, W -> W`.
   169	
   170	The new polynomials need not obey every optional coefficient gauge of
   171	the original parameter chart. That is immaterial for this interface:
   172	the permitted translation preserves Keller-ness, all listed published
   173	case data, and supplies precisely the missing upper-envelope (U).
   174	It is not asserted to be a unit certificate for the ambient source
   175	ideal, nor does it erase any retained external proof/certificate debt.
