    73	**3. Every leading-degree case at infinity.**
    74	
    75	Assume b nonzero and normalize b=1. Neither P nor L is zero because of its constant jet. Write `a=deg L`, `c=deg P`, `A=lc L`, and `C=lc P`. The complete list of potentially contributing degrees in F is
    76	
    77	```text
    78	2c, 2a+c, a+c, c+1, 4a, 3a, 2a+1, a+2, 3.
    79	```
    80	
    81	A vanished B or eta deletes terms from this list, never creates a new leader. Equal degrees among lower terms can cancel freely; the argument uses a unique leader only in the cases where it is strictly above all the others.
    82	
    83	| case | complete degree conclusion |
    84	|---|---|
    85	| L=0 or P=0 | impossible from the nonzero constant jets |
    86	| a=0, c>=2 | L=-1; `(theta-3)P^2` has the unique highest degree 2c, coefficient `(2c-3)C^2!=0`; all other degrees are at most c+1 or 3; impossible |
    87	| a=0, c=1 | jets give `P=-1/4-Bx`, B nonzero, eta=0; this is C_B |
    88	| a=0, c=0 | jets give B=eta=0 and P=-1/4; the common constant point |
