   230	**7. The b=0 stratum: a proved complementary classification and the missing boundary theorem.**
   231	
   232	First there is a complete whole-polynomial result on **b=B=0**. If L=0, (UF) gives `(theta-3)P^2=0`, hence P=0 in characteristic zero. Otherwise let m=deg L>=2. Top balance still gives `deg P=2m`, and
   233	
   234	```text
   235	P*(2xP'-3P+(3/2)L^2)=(3/16)L^4.                           (Z)
   236	```
   237	
   238	Suppose P has a nonzero root alpha of multiplicity r. Then L has multiplicity s>=1 there. In the differential factor the possible lowest orders are r-1 and 2s. Every possibility is as follows:
   239	
   240	| comparison | forced order of the product | contradiction |
   241	|---|---|---|
   242	| r-1<2s | 2r-1 | cannot equal the even number 4s |
   243	| r-1>2s | r+2s | equality to 4s would give r=2s, inconsistent with this inequality |
   244	| r-1=2s | at least r+2s=4s+1 | too large, including any cancellation of the two leaders |
   245	
   246	Thus P has no nonzero root, so `P=c*x^(2m)`. After division by x^(4m), (Z) is a constant quadratic equation for the rational function `L^2/x^(2m)`. That rational function must be constant. Consequently the complete list of nonzero solutions on b=B=0 is
   247	
   248	```text
   249	L=A*x^m, P=p*A^2*x^(2m), A!=0,
   250	(4m-3)p^2+(3/2)p-3/16=0.
   251	```
   252	
   253	In particular eta=P_2=0. These unbounded-degree solutions are outside the desired B*eta-nonzero boundary and illustrate why it would be wrong to exclude the entire b=0 stratum by leading balance.
